[Student_Question]
I'm trying to read a file in C++ like I did in C, but I keep hearing about RAII and exceptions. I don't get why my fopen/fclose approach is a problem. It works fine, right?

TA: 
Your code works in the simple case, but C++ emphasizes exception safety and automatic resource management. If an exception occurs between fopen and fclose, the file never gets closed—this is a resource leak. In C++, we use RAII: resources are acquired in constructors and released in destructors. For files, std::ifstream automatically closes the file when it goes out of scope, even during an exception. Try rewriting your code using std::ifstream and std::getline. How might that simplify cleanup?
